From 2c109bb94cd478dd4f1d448cc8ea39a5446b3b28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: keyspark <kevin.on.the.rocks@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 13:45:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] DSpark port: decode-shaped q8 microbatch kernel for
 verifier batches
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

For n_tok > 1 the q8_0 matmuls dispatched to cuBLAS over a dequantized
f16/f32 weight cache (2-4 bytes/weight) — right for compute-bound prefill
chunks, a 1.7-1.8x bandwidth tax on 2-16 row speculative verifier batches
where decode's native q8 GEMV reads 1.06 bytes/weight.  The native batch
fallback was no better: blockIdx.y per token re-streams the full matrix.

matmul_q8_0_preq_mrow_warp8_kernel keeps the decode kernel's shape (warp
per output row) and dots every prequantized activation row against each
weight block from registers, so the model is streamed once for the whole
batch.  A grouped variant covers the attention out_a path.  Dispatched for
2..16-row batches via --dspark-mbatch (DS4_CUDA_Q8_MBATCH env; no GPU API
change, Metal untouched).

GB10 measured: verify(2) 124 -> 95.5 ms, verify(5) 197 -> 167 ms;
extrapolated verify(1) ~= 72 ms ~= plain decode, i.e. the verifier's fixed
overhead is gone.  The remaining ~24 ms/row slope is routed-MoE expert
divergence between draft rows (the sorted expert-tile path already merges
duplicate experts), not kernel overhead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
